    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d11">

                <pb facs="0023" n="9" xml:id="pg9"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">825.24/312: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bowers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-02-04T19:00:00-04:00">February 4, 1942—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>208. See War Department’s telegram no. 68, January 31 to Military Attaché
                        here.<anchor n="24" xml:id="d11-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Have just had a talk with Rossetti who urged that any war material to be sent
                    should arrive coincident with reopening of Congress last week in February to
                    consider Rio resolution for breaking relations with Axis Powers. Properly
                    publicized here this should help remove opposition to ratification. Rossetti
                    said however that if any American military personnel accompanies material this
                    fact should be carefully handled and it should be emphasized that they were
                    merely coming for temporary training purposes.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d11-f1" n="24" xml:id="d11-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in this telegram the War Department indicated its consideration of
                    sending four batteries to Chile.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
